- op: discuss  # eca5
  at: '2026-04-07T09:49:27Z'  # eca5
  by: agent  # eca5
  actor: jgstern_agent  # eca5
  clock: 5  # eca5
  nonce: eca5  # eca5
  message: "Investigated codebase + ADRs + older tracker history. Answer: this is option (b) — a real implementation gap, not a rename.\n\nHISTORY: WI-fufuk (PR #2248, March 2026) added --dataflow with explicit direction-awareness — your follow-up 'what about reverse slicing?' produced commit 5a1a886b which made forward = follow write/mutate, reverse = follow read. So the directional design IS what you remembered ('data flow implies a direction implies discerning reads from writes') and was deliberately chosen.\n\nINV-jahov (PR #2634, late March) fixed a SEPARATE problem: reads were never being emitted at all (everything was 'write'). After that fix, repos started showing 24-41% access_mode coverage with both reads and writes emitted.\n\nWHAT THE REFLECT AGENT IS ACTUALLY COMPLAINING ABOUT (now distinguishable from the older issues): forward dataflow slicing currently chains writers→writers strictly, never crossing to readers. The 474 read edges that 'have dst nodes in the slice but are not admitted' means: there ARE reader nodes in the slice (reached via writer chains), and read-typed edges touch them, but the forward BFS skips read edges by design.\n\nWHAT ADR-0015 §6 ACTUALLY SAYS: 'BFS only follows edges where a write/mutate at the source connects to a read at the destination'. That phrase 'connects to a read at the destination' implies the slice should admit read edges when they are downstream of a write, not exclude them entirely. The implementation only checks the source's access_mode (slice.py:537-544), so it cannot distinguish 'write source → read dest' from 'write source → write dest'. The 'dest_access_mode' field that ADR-0015 §3 envisioned is defined but not populated by linkers.\n\nDESIGN VERDICT: do NOT rename to slice.sideeffects.0. The intent and ADR are clear: forward dataflow = data flows OUT (write site → downstream reads of what was written). The current implementation ships HALF of that: it follows writers but never crosses the write/read boundary. The fix is one of three options:\n\n(1) SIMPLE: reflect-agent's 'one-hop downstream read from each writer' rule. After reaching a writer node W via BFS, also admit one hop along W's outgoing read edges. Conservative, matches the §6 description, easy to implement (~15 lines in slice.py BFS), would roughly triple the prometheus dataflow slice.\n\n(2) PRINCIPLED: populate edge.meta.dest_access_mode at edge creation time (linker work), then in BFS check both src and dst modes — admit edges where (forward: src in {write,mutate} OR dst in {read,mutate}). Larger lift across linkers, but eliminates the per-node 'are we downstream of a writer?' bookkeeping.\n\n(3) STATEFUL: track per-node 'arrived via writer chain?' state in BFS, then admit reads only from writer-chain nodes. Cleaner than (1), no linker changes needed, but adds an axis to the visited set.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: option (1) for the immediate fix because it's small, contained to slice.py, and matches the reflect agent's empirically-grounded 'roughly triple the slice' estimate. Option (2) is the right longer-term direction but requires touching every linker that creates dataflow-relevant edges.\n\nCONNECTION TO INV-sabum: that item is stuck on the same root cause — neither item can be fully resolved without admitting cross-mode edges into the dataflow slice. If we fix WI-saful with option (1), INV-sabum's 'dispatches_to access_mode propagation' becomes much less impactful (the slice is already crossing read edges, so propagation matters less). I think both should be addressed together, with WI-saful done first (it has higher impact and lower architectural risk).\n\nI can implement option (1) under WI-saful as soon as you give the go-ahead. It's a 1-PR change with tests."  # eca5
